  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 105 AM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nSunday night through Friday:\n\nWarm air advection on southwest winds should occur Sunday night\ninto Monday across the area, ahead of the weakening low pressure\nsystem and southeastward-moving cold front. 925 mb temperatures\nmay rise into the 22 degree Celsius level, which may allow for\nhighs into the lower 80s or perhaps higher. The record highs for\nMonday are 83 degrees at Milwaukee and Madison. \n\nThe low and cold front may sag southeastward into the area later\nMonday or more likely Monday night, then to the southeast on\nTuesday. Dew points may rise into the lower to middle 60s Monday\nahead of the front, with some mean layer CAPE developing per\nforecast soundings. Deep layer bulk shear values are modest, so\nwill need to monitor later Monday into Monday night for chances\nfor showers and thunderstorms. Too early to say if any stronger\nstorms may occur, but it will bear watching over the next few\ndays. \n\nHigh pressure moving in behind the front Tuesday into Wednesday\nshould bring a brief period of quiet weather to the area, with\ncooler but still mild temperatures. \n\nTrends for later next week remain uncertain, with some ensembles\nshowing the potential for active weather and others less so.\nThis will depend on the track of the strong low pressure system\nand associated 500 mb trough shifting southeast across south \ncentral to southeast Canada. For now, kept the 20 to 50 percent\nchances for showers at times through the latter portions of next\nweek. Ensemble members still hint at a return to more \nseasonable and cooler temperatures by the end of next week and \ninto next weekend. \n\nWood",
